WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.490
Welcome to The Last Bookstore,

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:22.230
a sprawling shop in Los
Angeles where the written word

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.430
is still alive.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.430
At 22,000 square feet,
it's the largest bookstore

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.610
in the entire state of California.

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:30.110
You can get almost anything here,

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:31.750
but they're most famous
for their collection

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.330
of rare and expensive books,

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:34.750
and we're here today to find out how much

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.100
one of those pricey
page-turners will cost you.

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.516
Katie Orphan: There are
a lot of factors that go

00:00:40.516 --> 00:00:41.932
into a book's rarity,

00:00:41.932 --> 00:00:44.640
and there are a few different
ways that it happens.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.000
Obviously, titles that become significant.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.070
So, you've got things like
"The Sound and the Fury"

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:53.360
or "The Grapes of Wrath,"
titles that are older

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.240
but people value highly,
still talk about, still read.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.890
If you can find an intact first edition,

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:02.810
first printing with a jacket,
that's worth a lot of money

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:06.090
because that book is
significant to someone.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.930
If a title itself, or
an author themselves,

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.170
becomes precious to a reader,

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.250
that can increase the value of a book

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.770
because that reader is
willing to spend a lot

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:17.270
to have an important artifact.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.610
Sydney Kramer: They carry
anything from contemporary titles

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.130
to rare, out-of-print copies.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.097
This first edition of Ernest Hemingway's

00:01:25.097 --> 00:01:26.696
"The Sun Also Rises"

00:01:26.696 --> 00:01:28.529
costs $1,200.

00:01:28.529 --> 00:01:32.750
The cost is partially due
to an errata on page 181,

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:36.210
where the word "stopped"
is spelled with three Ps.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:37.555
This is all so cool, I'm like,

00:01:37.555 --> 00:01:40.250
"Oh, my God, was that, like,
a first edition Jack Kerouac?"

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:41.083
"Is that a first edition

00:01:41.083 --> 00:01:42.391
William S. Burroughs?"
Orphan: Yes.

00:01:42.391 --> 00:01:43.224
Orphan: Yes.

00:01:43.224 --> 00:01:45.150
Kramer: Oh, my God.
Orphan: Yes, to both.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.700
Kramer: The most expensive book sold

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.810
at The Last Bookstore was a
signed, limited-edition copy

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.350
of Jack Kerouac's "Visions of Cody."

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:55.033
It cost $2,300.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.800
Customer: We've been
here a few times before,

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.320
but it seems really cool,
it's a really good atmosphere,

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:03.700
especially for sitting down reading

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.633
and finding thousands of new books.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:09.307
Kramer: Here's a book about me.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:11.770
This is great, and all the people here

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:15.170
are just so appreciative
of having the opportunity

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:17.110
to really kind of look
through all these books.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.540
I mean, even if you go into
a standard bookstore now,

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.440
they don't have a selection like this.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:26.143
So, the question is: Are
rare books a good investment?

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:31.410
Orphan: I think, in the same
way that an investment in art

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:34.610
is a little bit risky, you
don't know what artists

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:38.380
are going to become major artists later on

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:40.930
and whose work will increase
in value and whose work

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.250
will just sort of stay at
the same level of value,

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:44.920
I think books are similar.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.270
There are some books that are always going

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:50.530
to have a lot of value
associated with them.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:53.680
You know, if we're talking,
like, "First Folio" Shakespeare,

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:57.470
first edition Charles Dickens,
first edition Jane Austen,

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:00.730
those, I think, are
going to have a stable,

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:02.423
if not increasing, value.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.120
Kramer: Collecting rare
and expensive books

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.500
definitely isn't for
everyone, but that's not all

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.070
they're known for here
at The Last Bookstore.

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.240
They have an entire section of $1 books,

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.280
so you can really get
anything you're looking for

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:14.530
and discover something new.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:16.230
The staff is extremely helpful,

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:18.730
and everyone here seems
really eager to share

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.430
the love of reading with
anyone who walks in the door.

